    rdf:value """she thinks of every hour and longs to possess “body, soul and spirit.” “If it were not for Willie and my Parents, I should make a sudden end to my love, but as it is I try to bear my anguish as secretly and graciously as may be,” i.e., by going out to a dozen teas every day, Miss Lowndes says. 
Sunday March 14. 1897.
I had the “weeping sinew” cut out of my hand, under chloroform, tied on a couple of tables in Janet’s rooms. It did not hurt, I did not hear what was being said, and yet, in some queer way, I had an  impression  """^^xsd:string ;
